{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1093","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1093","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1093\",\"parent_revision\":\"4ebd824dac52668950168ab16b04987ef7e9ab0923c9eba259525f90b78ccdc5\",\"root_revision\":\"4ebd824dac52668950168ab16b04987ef7e9ab0923c9eba259525f90b78ccdc5\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe `complete-implementation` skill recurses on follow-up tasks with only textual guidance preventing infinite loops. No programmatic recursion depth limit or cycle detection exists. Runaway quality gate recursion is possible when follow-up tasks themselves generate further follow-ups without bound.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: Add anti-loop guard to complete-implementation recursive follow-up handling\\nGoal: Add DH_RECURSIVE_REVIEW_TASK_DEPTH cap, RT-ICA BLOCKED stop, and in/out-of-scope\\n      routing to the Recursive Follow-up Handling section of complete-implementation\\n\\nConditions:\\n1. complete-implementation SKILL.md exists and is readable\\n   Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Fact-checker read file, verified lines 645-674\\n\\n2. Recursive Follow-up Handling section exists in the skill\\n   Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Fact-checker VERIFIED lines 535-670\\n\\n3. Step 4 Recursion Gate logic is present\\n   Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Fact-checker VERIFIED lines 645-674\\n\\n4. DH_RECURSIVE_REVIEW_TASK_DEPTH value (5) and placement agreed\\n   Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Discovery doc confirmed by user\\n\\n5. In-scope vs out-of-scope classification criteria defined\\n   Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Discovery doc confirmed by user\\n\\n6. RT-ICA BLOCKED stop condition agreed\\n   Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Discovery doc confirmed by user\\n\\n7. backlog_add MCP pattern exists for out-of-scope routing\\n   Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Fact-checker VERIFIED at line 321\\n\\n8. No other skill files depend on complete-implementation's recursion internals\\n   Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Impact-analyst \u2014 implement-feature and\\n   work-backlog-item invoke the skill but do not depend on its recursion logic internals\\n\\n9. Existing test coverage for the recursion path\\n   Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Impact-analyst \u2014 test_scenarios.py has\\n   ZERO coverage for depth limit and RT-ICA BLOCKED paths (HIGH RISK, in-scope for this task)\\n\\n10. ADR-2 and ADR-3 definitions referenced in Step 4 are intact\\n    Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Fact-checker VERIFIED lines 645-674\\n\\n11. (new) code-reviewer.md needs scope classification guidance\\n    Snapshot: \u2014 \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Impact-analyst MEDIUM RISK system; added to\\n    Acceptance Criteria and Files sections\\n\\n12. (new) backlog-item-lifecycle.md documents recursion behavior and will become stale\\n    Snapshot: \u2014 \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Impact-analyst MEDIUM RISK system; added to\\n    Acceptance Criteria and Files sections\\n\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 8: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (impact-analyst confirmed no external dependency on internals)\\n- Condition 9: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (impact-analyst confirmed zero coverage \u2014 HIGH RISK flag)\\n- Condition 11: (new) AVAILABLE \u2014 discovered by impact-analyst\\n- Condition 12: (new) AVAILABLE \u2014 discovered by impact-analyst\\n\\nFact-check note: Original backlog description cited \\\"3 cycles without progress\\\" guidance as\\nresiding in the skill \u2014 REFUTED. That text is in .claude/rules/fix-delegation-discipline.md.\\nThe skill has no cycle-count reference. This does not affect the feature scope.\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED \u2014 all 12 conditions AVAILABLE, 0 MISSING.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nQuality gate recursion can run unbounded if follow-up tasks continuously spawn additional follow-ups. Currently only textual instructions (\\\"after 3 cycles without progress, report BLOCKED\\\") prevent this \u2014 no programmatic enforcement.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md::Recursive Follow-up Handling Step 4` - Defines the recursion gate logic that will be modified to include depth limit and RT-ICA BLOCKED condition. Change adds DH_RECURSIVE_REVIEW_TASK_DEPTH constant (value: 5) and new stop condition. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Central control logic touched; affects all downstream recursion behavior; requires validation that depth counter persists across cycles and RT-ICA BLOCKED is correctly detected.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md::Step 3 Link or Create Backlog Item` - Will be modified to route out-of-scope findings via `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_add` (new in-scope vs out-of-scope classification). This introduces a new consumer of backlog_add with a specific call pattern. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: New MCP tool invocation pattern; must verify backlog_add accepts the calling pattern (no preview/auto mode used); error handling for creation failures must be defined.\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` - Orchestrator calls `complete-implementation` at end of implement-feature workflow. The skill assumes recursion depth has no hard limit. After this change, recursion will halt at depth 5. No code change needed, but behavior changes: long follow-up chains will now terminate programmatically instead of relying on textual guidance. | Risk: LOW | Why: No code change required; orchestrator already rerunning complete-implementation as needed; depth limit is a safety guard, not a breaking change. Agent instructions may need clarification (see Agent Instructions section).\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` - Invokes complete-implementation when a feature backlog item enters the quality gates phase. Recursion depth limit affects how many times a feature can loop through quality gates automatically before being deferred to backlog. No code change, but documentation should clarify what \\\"depth exceeded\\\" means for user. | Risk: LOW | Why: Existing behavior preserved (defer to backlog on depth limit, same as existing ADR-2/ADR-3 deferral); no functional breakage.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/start-task/SKILL.md` - May indirectly trigger complete-implementation via task completion. No direct change needed. | Risk: LOW | Why: Indirect dependency; depth limit applies at complete-implementation level, not task level.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` - Contains `backlog_add` MCP operation implementation. The new routing logic in Step 3 will invoke this with out-of-scope findings. Must verify: (1) backlog_add accepts the calling signature without preview mode, (2) error handling for cases where item creation fails but follow-up file already created. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: New call pattern from complete-implementation; MCP tool must reliably handle the new usage; if backlog_add fails silently, follow-up files are orphaned with no backlog item.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` - The Phase 1 agent that produces follow-up task files. After the change, follow-ups will be classified as in-scope (fixed immediately) or out-of-scope (new backlog item created). Agent instructions should clarify what \\\"in-scope\\\" means in the context of the new classification logic. No code change to the agent itself, but the instructions it follows will change. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Agent output (follow-up files) will be consumed by new in/out-of-scope classification logic; if agent generates follow-ups with unclear scope metadata, the classification step will need fallback heuristics; error paths must be tested.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/feature-verifier.md`, `plugins/development-harness/agents/integration-checker.md`, `plugins/development-harness/agents/doc-drift-auditor.md`, `plugins/development-harness/agents/context-refinement.md` - Agents in the quality gate dispatch loop (T2-T6). These agents do not generate follow-ups; they validate the implementation. No direct change needed. | Risk: LOW | Why: Recursion logic applies only to follow-ups generated by T1 (code-reviewer); these agents are unaffected.\\n\\n### Tests\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_scenarios.py` - Contains scenario tests for complete-implementation workflow. Must add test cases for: (1) depth limit enforcement (5+ recursive cycles should terminate), (2) RT-ICA BLOCKED stop condition (recursion halts when RT-ICA blocks), (3) in-scope vs out-of-scope routing (follow-ups with different scope values create backlog items correctly). | Risk: HIGH | Why: No test coverage currently exists for the new depth limit or RT-ICA BLOCKED condition; without tests, silent failures are possible (depth counter not incremented, BLOCKED condition never checked); the new backlog_add routing logic must be validated end-to-end.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests_sam/test_*.py` - SAM plan tests cover task plan operations. If depth limit is tracked in plan metadata (recommended), SAM plan tests should verify that recursion depth is correctly incremented and available to complete-implementation. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Recursion depth counter must be stored somewhere persistent across complete-implementation runs; if stored in plan metadata, SAM tests must validate the counter is read/written correctly; if stored in transient context, recursion depth is lost on re-run.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/tests/test_task_status_hook/` - Tests for task status hook that marks tasks complete. If complete-implementation is triggered as a post-execution hook, depth limit must not interfere with normal task lifecycle. | Risk: LOW | Why: Task lifecycle is orthogonal to recursion depth; depth limit applies at feature level, not task level.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` - References the complete-implementation quality gates phase and recursive follow-up handling. Will become stale if it describes recursion without mentioning the depth limit (value: 5) and RT-ICA BLOCKED stop condition. Must be updated to state: (1) follow-ups halt at depth 5, (2) RT-ICA BLOCKED halts recursion, (3) out-of-scope findings create new backlog items via backlog_add. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Documentation is consulted by orchestrators and agents to understand recursion behavior; stale documentation will cause incorrect expectations about when recursion stops and what happens to out-of-scope findings.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-architecture-diagram.md` - High-level workflow diagram. If it depicts the recursion loop, must add notation showing depth limit and BLOCKED stop condition. | Risk: LOW | Why: Diagram is high-level; adding a note about depth limit is straightforward; no behavioral impact if diagram is not updated immediately.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/process-audit-backlog-lifecycle-2026-03-02.md` - Process audit document. Likely contains observations about recursion behavior. May reference the old \\\"only textual guidance\\\" limitation. Should be updated to note the new programmatic guards. | Risk: LOW | Why: Audit document is historical reference; updating it improves future context but does not affect runtime behavior.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/README.md` - Plugin overview. If it mentions quality gates and recursion, should note that recursion is now bounded. | Risk: LOW | Why: README is user-facing overview; impact is minimal if not updated immediately.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` - The main instruction file being modified. Will explicitly document the new depth limit and RT-ICA BLOCKED condition in Step 4 (Recursion Gate) and Step 3 (Link or Create Backlog Item). Documentation update is integral to the change (not separate). | Risk: LOW | Why: This is the primary change deliverable; updating the SKILL.md is the core task.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n- `.github/workflows/quality-gate-*.yml` (if any) - If CI workflows invoke complete-implementation, they should be aware that recursion depth limit applies. Most likely, CI workflows are orthogonal to the human-facing orchestration, so no change needed. | Risk: LOW | Why: CI workflows typically run specific test suites, not the full orchestration loop; depth limit applies to human workflow orchestration.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/plugin.json` - No configuration of the skill itself is needed; depth limit is a constant in SKILL.md, not a config parameter. | Risk: LOW | Why: No plugin-level configuration change needed.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` - Should clarify that follow-up task files produced by this agent may be classified as in-scope (fixed in current cycle) or out-of-scope (new backlog item). If the agent is to understand what \\\"scope\\\" means in the context of the change, instructions must explain the classification criteria (linting/tests/docs/same design goals = in-scope; separate system/high impact = out-of-scope). Current instructions do not mention scope classification. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Agent may currently produce follow-ups without metadata indicating scope; the new classification logic will need to infer scope or use heuristics; if agent instructions are not updated to indicate scope intention, classification errors are possible.\\n\\n- Instructions for quality gate agents (T1-T6) - If any of these agents are aware of the recursion mechanism, they should note that recursion now halts at depth 5 and when RT-ICA BLOCKED. Most likely, agents treat recursion as an orchestration concern (not their responsibility), so no update needed. | Risk: LOW | Why: Recursion is handled by complete-implementation orchestrator, not individual agents; agents need not be aware of depth limit.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n| Path | Role | Connection | Action | Risk |\\n|------|------|-----------|--------|------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` | producer | Defines recursion gate and follow-up routing logic; being modified to add depth limit and RT-ICA BLOCKED stop | CODE_CHANGE | MEDIUM |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` | producer | Implements backlog_add MCP operation; new call pattern from complete-implementation Step 3 | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | MEDIUM |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` | consumer | Invokes complete-implementation at end of workflow; recursion behavior changes | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | LOW |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` | consumer | Invokes complete-implementation for quality gates; depth limit affects loop termination | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | LOW |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` | agent-instruction | Produces follow-up files consumed by recursion gate; scope classification criteria needed | AGENT_UPDATE | MEDIUM |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` | documentation | Documents complete-implementation recursion phase; must note depth limit and BLOCKED stop | CONTENT_UPDATE | MEDIUM |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_scenarios.py` | test | Scenario tests for complete-implementation workflow; missing test coverage for depth limit and BLOCKED condition | TEST_UPDATE | HIGH |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/references/` (if exists) | documentation | Reference files explaining recursion gates and ADR-2/ADR-3; may need depth limit clarification | CONTENT_UPDATE | LOW |\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- **Overall system risk**: MEDIUM\\n- **Highest-risk systems**:\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_scenarios.py` - No existing test coverage for the new depth limit or RT-ICA BLOCKED condition; runaway recursion could occur undetected if implementation is incorrect\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md::Step 3 Link or Create Backlog Item` - New backlog_add routing pattern must be compatible with existing MCP tool and handle failures gracefully\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` - Agent instructions must clarify scope classification criteria or new logic will misclassify follow-ups\\n\\n- **Main risk themes**:\\n  - **Verification risk**: No test coverage for depth limit enforcement or RT-ICA BLOCKED condition; silent failures possible if depth counter not persisted\\n  - **Compatibility risk**: New backlog_add call pattern must be validated against existing MCP operation signature; error handling for failed backlog item creation must be defined\\n  - **Documentation risk**: Agent instructions and lifecycle docs become stale if not updated to explain scope classification and depth limit behavior\\n  - **Behavioral risk**: Follow-up routing changes semantics (some findings now create backlog items instead of fixing immediately); this is intentional but requires clear documentation\\n\\n- **Scope expansion**:\\n  - **Initial scope**: Modify Recursion Gate Step 4 with depth limit and RT-ICA BLOCKED condition\\n  - **Discovered scope**: Step 3 (Link or Create Backlog Item) also modified to route out-of-scope findings via backlog_add (new MCP pattern)\\n  - **Downstream scope**: Code-reviewer agent instructions should clarify scope classification; lifecycle docs must document new behavior; test coverage must validate both depth limit and RT-ICA BLOCKED\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Depth limit constant DH_RECURSIVE_REVIEW_TASK_DEPTH (value: 5) defined and validated in SKILL.md\\n- [ ] RT-ICA BLOCKED stop condition checked and documented in Step 4 Recursion Gate\\n- [ ] In-scope vs out-of-scope classification criteria defined in Step 3 and communicated to code-reviewer agent\\n- [ ] backlog_add MCP call pattern verified compatible with existing operation\\n- [ ] backlog_add error handling (creation failure, follow-up file orphaning) tested and documented\\n- [ ] Recursion depth counter persistence mechanism verified (stored in plan metadata or persistent context)\\n- [ ] Test coverage added for: (1) depth limit enforcement (5+ cycles halt), (2) RT-ICA BLOCKED stop, (3) in-scope routing creates no new item, (4) out-of-scope routing creates backlog item\\n- [ ] Agent instructions updated: code-reviewer clarifies scope classification criteria\\n- [ ] Documentation updated: backlog-item-lifecycle.md notes depth limit and BLOCKED stop condition\\n- [ ] No stale references to \\\"only textual guidance\\\" preventing loops in any docs or comments\\n- [ ] Recursion depth counter reset between complete-implementation invocations (per feature, not global)\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked: does depth limit affect other orchestration workflows (e.g., milestone dispatch, multi-phase planning)?\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-03-30)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\nThe infinite loop condition can be demonstrated by invoking `/dh:complete-implementation` on a task where the code-reviewer agent (T1) repeatedly generates follow-up findings that meet the ADR-3 scope match and ADR-2 high-priority conditions. Under the current skill:\\n\\n1. `/dh:complete-implementation` runs the quality gate loop (T1-T6)\\n2. T1 (code-reviewer) produces a follow-up task file\\n3. Step 4 Recursion Gate checks: (a) follow-up slug matches current task slug (ADR-3), and (b) follow-up is High priority (ADR-2)\\n4. Both conditions pass \u2192 skill re-invokes `implement-feature` then `complete-implementation` recursively\\n5. The re-invoked `complete-implementation` runs quality gates again \u2192 T1 produces another follow-up \u2192 conditions pass again\\n6. No depth counter exists in the skill to detect or halt this cycle\\n\\nThe loop continues until an external signal interrupts the session. There is no programmatic stop condition in `complete-implementation/SKILL.md` today.\\n\\n### Output / Evidence\\n\\nWhen complete, the following will be observable:\\n\\n1. `complete-implementation/SKILL.md` contains `DH_RECURSIVE_REVIEW_TASK_DEPTH = 5` with inline purpose comment\\n2. Step 4 Recursion Gate increments and checks depth before each recursive call; when exceeded, outputs a systemic design issue message with depth count and task identifier, then stops\\n3. Step 4 checks RT-ICA result before recursing; if BLOCKED, stops and surfaces blocking conditions to user\\n4. Step 3 classifies each finding as in-scope or out-of-scope; out-of-scope findings trigger `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_add` immediately and do not block the current cycle\\n5. `code-reviewer.md` includes scope classification criteria matching the discovery doc definition\\n6. `backlog-item-lifecycle.md` documents: depth limit (5), RT-ICA BLOCKED stop, and out-of-scope routing via backlog_add\\n7. Test cases in `tests/test_scenarios.py` cover: depth limit enforcement (5+ cycles halt), RT-ICA BLOCKED stop, in-scope routing (no new item created), out-of-scope routing (backlog item created)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\nP1 \u2014 Safety and correctness issue in the quality gate pipeline.\\n\\nThe recursion path has no programmatic stop condition. Any follow-up chain where both ADR-3 (scope match) and ADR-2 (high priority) conditions are continuously satisfied will run unbounded. The fix-delegation-discipline rule that mentions \\\"after 3 cycles without progress, report BLOCKED\\\" is textual guidance in `.claude/rules/fix-delegation-discipline.md` \u2014 it is not enforced in the skill itself (confirmed by Fact-Check). The quality gate pipeline is a core path executed on every feature implementation. An unguarded loop in this path is a correctness failure, not a degradation scenario.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\nWithout this fix:\\n\\n- A feature with continuously failing quality gate reviews enters an unbounded recursion loop with no automatic halt\\n- The only stop is external session interruption \u2014 no message is surfaced to the user about the loop depth or its cause\\n- Out-of-scope findings discovered during recursion have no routing path: they are either silently dropped or incorrectly fed back into the current cycle, blocking task completion\\n- RT-ICA BLOCKED results during recursion do not halt the loop \u2014 the skill re-invokes anyway, creating recursion against an unresolvable information gap\\n- No test coverage exists for any of these failure modes (Impact Radius: `tests/test_scenarios.py` \u2014 Risk: HIGH)\\n- `code-reviewer.md` agent has no scope classification criteria, so the in-scope/out-of-scope routing decision cannot be made correctly even after the guard is added\\n- `backlog-item-lifecycle.md` documents recursion behavior without mentioning a depth limit \u2014 orchestrators reading it will not know that recursion can be unbounded\\n\\n### Scope\\n\\n**In scope:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` \u2014 Step 4 (Recursion Gate): add `DH_RECURSIVE_REVIEW_TASK_DEPTH` constant (value: 5), depth increment and check at start of each recursive call, RT-ICA BLOCKED stop condition\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` \u2014 Step 3 (Link or Create Backlog Item): add in-scope vs out-of-scope classification and `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_add` routing for out-of-scope findings\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` \u2014 add scope classification criteria so the agent's follow-up files carry scope intent (Impact Radius: AGENT_UPDATE, Risk: MEDIUM)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` \u2014 document depth limit (value: 5), RT-ICA BLOCKED stop, and out-of-scope routing behavior (Impact Radius: CONTENT_UPDATE, Risk: MEDIUM)\\n\\n**Scope expansion discovered during Impact Radius analysis:**\\n- Step 3 modification (out-of-scope routing via `backlog_add`) was not in the initial scope statement \u2014 it is required to make the in/out-of-scope classification actionable\\n- `code-reviewer.md` agent update is required for correct classification at the point of follow-up generation\\n- `backlog-item-lifecycle.md` update is required to prevent orchestrator confusion about recursion termination\\n\\n**Anti-Goals (from discovery doc):**\\n- Does NOT change existing ADR-3 (scope match) gate condition\\n- Does NOT affect the non-recursive path (issue-only path, Proportional Quality Gates)\\n- Does NOT change the RT-ICA BLOCKED behavior elsewhere \u2014 only applies to the recursion path\\n- Does NOT persist depth count across separate top-level `/complete-implementation` invocations\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- [ ] `DH_RECURSIVE_REVIEW_TASK_DEPTH` constant (value: 5) is defined in `complete-implementation/SKILL.md` with purpose stated inline\\n- [ ] Depth is checked and incremented at the start of each recursive call in Step 4\\n- [ ] When depth exceeds `DH_RECURSIVE_REVIEW_TASK_DEPTH`: loop stops, systemic design issue message output with depth count and task identifier, remaining in-scope findings routed to backlog\\n- [ ] RT-ICA BLOCKED result during recursion stops the loop and surfaces blocking conditions to the user\\n- [ ] In-scope findings (linting, tests, docs, same design goals/intent/outcomes) are routed back into the current cycle for fix and re-verification\\n- [ ] Out-of-scope findings (separate system/domain, or high perceived impact warranting own grooming) trigger `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_add` immediately and do not block the current cycle\\n- [ ] `code-reviewer.md` agent instructions include scope classification criteria (in-scope: linting/tests/docs/same design goals; out-of-scope: separate system/domain or high-impact warranting own grooming)\\n- [ ] `backlog-item-lifecycle.md` documents the depth limit (value: 5), RT-ICA BLOCKED stop condition, and out-of-scope routing behavior\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n| File | Action | Risk |\\n|------|--------|------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` (Step 4 \u2014 Recursion Gate) | CODE_CHANGE \u2014 add depth limit constant, increment/check, RT-ICA BLOCKED stop | MEDIUM |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` (Step 3 \u2014 Link or Create Backlog Item) | CODE_CHANGE \u2014 add in/out-of-scope classification and backlog_add routing | MEDIUM |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` | AGENT_UPDATE \u2014 add scope classification criteria | MEDIUM |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` | CONTENT_UPDATE \u2014 document depth limit, BLOCKED stop, out-of-scope routing | MEDIUM |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_scenarios.py` | TEST_UPDATE \u2014 add depth limit, RT-ICA BLOCKED, in/out-of-scope routing test cases | HIGH |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE \u2014 confirm backlog_add call signature accepts new usage pattern | MEDIUM |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE \u2014 no code change; confirm depth limit does not break orchestration | LOW |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE \u2014 no code change; confirm loop termination behavior is documented | LOW |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Feature-context discovery artifact registered and confirmed current for issue #1093 (path: `plan/feature-context-anti-loop-guard-complete-implementation.md`). All design questions resolved \u2014 no open questions remain.\\n- `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_add` MCP tool is available and has an existing call pattern in `complete-implementation/SKILL.md` (line 321 \u2014 Concerns routing). New usage in Step 3 must be validated against that pattern before implementation.\\n- No external blockers. No dependency on other in-flight issues.\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 1-2 hours implementation.\\n\\n- Primary change: SKILL.md edits (Step 3 + Step 4) \u2014 targeted text additions to existing sections; no structural reorganization required\\n- Agent update: `code-reviewer.md` \u2014 add classification criteria block; no workflow change\\n- Doc update: `backlog-item-lifecycle.md` \u2014 add one paragraph or callout on depth limit and BLOCKED stop\\n- No Python code changes required \u2014 `backlog_add` call pattern already exists in the skill\\n- Test coverage: `test_scenarios.py` cases for depth limit and RT-ICA BLOCKED \u2014 effort is TBD and may exceed the skill edits; implementation team should scope test authorship separately if needed\\n- Total file count: 3 files modified (SKILL.md, code-reviewer.md, backlog-item-lifecycle.md) + 1 verified-compatible (operations.py) + 1 test file updated\\n\\n## Where Observed\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nIdentified during OpenSpace research insight extraction (`research/insights/2026-03-28-OpenSpace-improvements.md`). The insight extractor flagged this as a P1 pattern when analyzing OpenSpace's self-evolving skill loop mechanisms \u2014 the contrast highlighted the absence of a similar guard in `complete-implementation`.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Verbatim Source Report\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n> Add anti-loop guard